  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "The $10$th and $11$th terms of an arithmetic sequence are $30$ and $33,$ respectively. Determine the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence.",
  "worked_solution": "The formula for the $n$th term is given by \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nSince the terms ${a}_{10}=30$ and ${a}_{11}=33$ are consecutive, we can subtract them to find the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{11}-{a}_{10} \\\\  & =33-30 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, ${a}_{10}=30,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since ${a}_{10}$ is $9$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{10} & ={a}_{1}+9d \\\\ 30 & ={a}_{1}+9(3) \\\\ 30 & ={a}_{1}+27 \\\\ 30-27 & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence is:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =3+(n-1)(3) \\\\  & =3+3n-3 \\\\  & =3n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
